## Altitudes: which tool answers which question

Start at the system timeline unless you already have strong evidence the
bottleneck is kernel-local or library-selection. Descend only as far as the
evidence demands; each altitude below costs more overhead than the last.

| Altitude | Answers | Tools |
| -------- | ------- | ----- |
| System timeline | host/device overlap, idle gaps, launch cost, HIP graph coverage, library selection (AITER/CK/hipBLASLt/Triton/torch-native) | `files`, `kernels`, `families`, `idle_gaps`, `cpu_overhead`, `memory`, `graphs`, `host_idle`, `query`, `summary` |
| Counters (PMC) | which hardware ceiling one specific kernel is against | `counter_sets`, `counter_plan`, `counter_report`, `counter_triage` |
| Kernel-internal | full Speed-of-Light + roofline for one targeted kernel | `compute_doctor`, `compute_analyze` (capture via shell, see below) |
| Instruction-level | per-instruction stalls inside one kernel, one compute unit (version-gated, see Pitfalls) | `att_plan`, `att_hotspots` |
| Paired A/B | did a change actually move the needle, same session, drift-controlled | `bench_parse`, `bench_compare`, `bench_verdict` |

## Analysis toolkit: `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` MCP tools OR shell

If the `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` MCP server is attached to this round, call its tools directly (argument names shown). Otherwise, shell out to the matching subcommand on `python {{ profiler_support_name }}/<module>.py`.

### System trace (`analyze_rocprof.py`)

| MCP tool | Shell equivalent | Purpose |
| -------- | ---------------- | ------- |
| `files(report)` | `... analyze_rocprof.py files <report>` | Discover captured files, processes, agents; start here. |
| `kernels(report, top=15)` | `... kernels <report> --top 15` | Top GPU kernels by total time, with a library-family column. |
| `families(report)` | `... families <report>` | GPU time grouped by kernel library family (AITER/CK/hipBLASLt/Triton/torch-native). A fallback share where a fused-library share was expected means a silent fallback, not a hardware limit. |
| `idle_gaps(report, top=10)` | `... idle_gaps <report> --top 10` | Largest GPU idle gaps between kernel launches. |
| `cpu_overhead(report)` | `... cpu_overhead <report>` | HIP API launch overhead and launch-bound heuristic. |
| `memory(report)` | `... memory <report>` | Memory copies by direction, bytes, bandwidth. |
| `graphs(report)` | `... graphs <report>` | HIP graph launches and attribution-degradation check. |
| `host_idle(report)` | `... host_idle <report>` | Validity check: detects a mostly host-idle / load-missed capture. |
| `query(report, sql)` | `... query <report> "<SQL>"` | Arbitrary SQL against a rocpd SQLite export, if present (ROCm 7+). |
| `summary(report, top=15)` | `... summary <report>` | All-in-one: kernels + families + idle_gaps + cpu_overhead + memory + graphs. |

**Validity first.** Before trusting any bottleneck read, check `host_idle` (a
mostly-idle capture usually means you profiled the wrong process: the API
server/driver, not the worker issuing HIP kernels) and `graphs` (confirms
whether decode is running under a captured HIP graph or eager; the two need
different follow-up: replay timing and inter-replay gaps for graphed decode,
`idle_gaps`/`cpu_overhead` for eager).

### PMC counters (`counters.py`): one hardware ceiling for one hot kernel

| MCP tool | Shell equivalent | Purpose |
| -------- | ---------------- | ------- |
| `counter_sets(arch=None)` | `... counters.py list-sets --arch <arch>` | Print the counter-set catalogue for an architecture (or all). |
| `counter_plan(arch, sets, kernel=None, out_dir="rocprof_pmc", command=None)` | `... plan --arch <arch> --sets <s1,s2> --kernel <regex> -- <command>` | Print one `rocprofv3 --pmc` command line per requested counter set. |
| `counter_report(dirs, kernel=None, top=15, arch=None)` | `... report <dir1> <dir2> ...` | Merge PMC passes per kernel; print resource usage + derived metrics (L2 hit rate, HBM bandwidth, MFMA issue rate, LDS bank-conflict rate). |
| `counter_triage(dirs, arch, kernel=None, top=15)` | `... triage <dir1> <dir2> ... --arch <arch>` | Classify each hot kernel's bottleneck (occupancy/bandwidth/compute/LDS/launch/latency) with evidence and a next lever. |

**Use `counter_plan`; never hand-write a `--pmc` list.** Keep each pass to
**at most 4 counters**; a job that forces multi-pass collection has been
reported to trigger a GPU hang on CDNA hardware. Give each requested counter
set **its own output directory**; never reuse an output directory across
passes, and never combine sets into one `--pmc` invocation. Target one
already-identified hot kernel; this is not a whole-run tool.

### Kernel-internal (`compute.py`, rocprof-compute)

| MCP tool | Shell equivalent | Purpose |
| -------- | ---------------- | ------- |
| `compute_doctor()` | `... compute.py doctor` | Diagnose the rocprof-compute install (binary, deps interpreter, pandas version, the deprecated legacy `rocprof` it actually shells out to, aqlprofile) before spending a capture on it. |
| `compute_analyze(workload_dir, blocks=..., max_stat=10, kernel="", timeout=300)` | `... analyze <workload_dir>` | Run `rocprof-compute analyze`; falls back to a summarized read of the raw CSVs (top kernels by time, a couple of rough counter ratios) if it fails. |

`compute.py profile` (the capture step) is long-running and stays **shell
command, not an MCP tool**, same reasoning as `rocprofv3` capture itself. Run
`compute_doctor` first: `rocprof-compute` refuses to run unless its Python
dependency set is importable in whichever interpreter runs it, an
all-or-nothing preflight you want to hit before, not during, a capture. Then
capture with a hard timeout, targeted at one already-identified kernel, never
a whole run:

```
python {{ profiler_support_name }}/compute.py profile --name <workload> --out /tmp/rocprof_compute \
  --kernel <regex> --timeout 900 -- <command>
```

**`--kernel` matches a literal substring, not a friendly name: verify it
first.** Confirmed on a real capture: torch GEMMs dispatch through
rocBLAS/hipBLASLt as Tensile kernels named like
`Cijk_Ailk_Bljk_BBS_BH_..._MT256x128x32_...`, which does **not** contain the
substring `"gemm"`: a naive `--kernel gemm` filter matches zero dispatches,
and `profile` here now fails loudly with the fix (list real kernel names with
`rocprofv3 --kernel-trace --stats` first) instead of forwarding rocprof-
compute's own confusing `KeyError: 'Grid_Size'` crash.

### Instruction-level (`att.py`, Advanced Thread Trace): version-gated

| MCP tool | Shell equivalent | Purpose |
| -------- | ---------------- | ------- |
| `att_plan(arch, kernel, target_cu=1, ..., command=None)` | `... att.py plan --arch <arch> --kernel <regex> -- <command>` | Print a rocprofv3 ATT job config and the invocation to run it. |
| `att_hotspots(dispatch_dir, top=15)` | `... hotspots <dispatch_dir>` | Top stall hotspots (instructions + source lines) from a decoded ATT dispatch directory. |

ATT needs a `rocprofv3` >= ROCm 7.1 (the `--att`/thread-trace flag doesn't
exist before that) and the separate `rocprof-trace-decoder` shared library,
which ships as its own GitHub release, not inside any ROCm module. Run
`att_plan` early: it prints the exact working command line, including
`--att-library-path`, once you know where the decoder `.so` is. A decoder-path
error means it isn't installed or isn't pointed at yet: the library is a
single small `.so` extractable from the release tarball with no build step
and no root, so try installing it into a scratch directory before giving up
on ATT for the session; if that isn't practical in the time available, treat
the error as "ATT unavailable on this host," not a usage bug, and fall back
to `counters.py`/`compute.py` for hardware-ceiling evidence instead. Only
reach for ATT after counters already point at a specific stall class; it
attributes per-instruction stalls inside one kernel, one compute unit, and
costs the most overhead of any tool here.

### Paired A/B (`kernel_bench.py`): did the change actually help

| MCP tool | Shell equivalent | Purpose |
| -------- | ---------------- | ------- |
| `bench_parse(log)` | `... kernel_bench.py parse <log>` | Extract `wall_ms: <float>` lines from a driver log into `{"wall_ms": [...]}`. |
| `bench_compare(file_a, file_b, threshold_pct=3.0)` | `... compare <file_a> <file_b>` | Unpaired median comparison of two recorded (non-interleaved) sample files. |
| `bench_verdict(samples, threshold_pct=3.0)` | `... verdict <samples>` | Paired A/B verdict from a file of genuinely interleaved recorded pairs, the preferred shape for "did this help." |

This verifies; it does not diagnose. Use it to confirm a hypothesis already
localized by the tools above, same session, drift-controlled (alternate
reference/candidate reps, never compare across sessions or boxes).

## Capturing a profile (shell)

Capture is a long-running shell step; do it before calling the MCP tools.

1. Read the objective, candidate contract, manifest, build/startup commands, and benchmark `--help` output to understand which implementation and process the workload exercises{% if benchmark_command is defined and benchmark_command %}: `{{ benchmark_command }} --help`{% endif %}. Do not assume a filename, language, or launcher.
2. Stop any stale candidate processes from previous attempts.
3. Pre-warm under load (CRITICAL on ROCm: first-time AITER/Triton JIT resolution and any `TunableOp`/AITER online tuning take minutes and must not land inside the captured window). This is also where HIP-graph capture happens for graph-mode serving; keep it outside the traced window so the trace holds only steady-state replay kernels, not one-time capture kernels.
4. Identify the process that actually issues HIP kernels. vLLM's engine core and SGLang's per-TP-rank workers run in a process separate from the API server/driver; a driver-only capture shows near-zero GPU activity by construction: that is "wrong process," not "the GPU is idle." For tensor-parallel serving, capture each worker independently. **`rocprofv3` only flushes its trace buffers on a clean process exit**: killing a wrapped server (SIGTERM/SIGKILL) loses the trace even though the run otherwise looked fine, because the engine-core subprocess vLLM/SGLang forks for serving does not exit cleanly on an external kill of the parent. Prefer an offline single-process script (e.g. `vllm.LLM(...).generate(...)` / SGLang's equivalent) that exits on its own once the workload finishes, over wrapping the long-running server. For vLLM specifically, set `VLLM_ENABLE_V1_MULTIPROCESSING=0` so the engine runs in-process (no forked `EngineCore` that can fail to exit cleanly). If a server must be captured, shut it down through its own graceful path, never a hard kill of the process tree.
5. Profile under representative load, with CSV output and enough trace kinds for every analysis above (kernel- and memory-copy-level events, plus aggregated stats):
   ```
   rocprofv3 --kernel-trace --memory-copy-trace --stats --output-format csv \
     -d /tmp/rocprof_profile -o capture \
     -- <offline script or server command>
   ```
   Use `--output-format csv` only: adding `json` duplicates every CSV's content inside one JSON blob and can reach GB scale at serving-workload row counts for zero extra information. Add `--hip-runtime-trace` only when `cpu_overhead`/`graphs`/launch-bound analysis is actually needed: it traces one HIP runtime API call per kernel launch/memcpy/event, so on a batched-decode workload it typically runs 2-4x the size of the kernel trace it's paired with; do not enable it by default on a large capture. Constrain the collection window to representative load: a multi-minute full trace is unreadable and wastes capture overhead. Bound every capture with a hard timeout, sized generously: the post-exit flush to disk can itself take minutes and scales with `--hip-runtime-trace` volume, not with capture duration, so a capture that exits promptly can still take a few minutes longer to finish writing files; do not treat "no output yet" right after exit as failure. Kill the whole process tree (not just the top PID) only on a genuine timeout, so a stuck worker does not orphan a HIP context for the next round. On a shared node, use a port scoped to this job rather than a fixed default, to avoid colliding with another concurrent run.
6. Wait for the trace files to appear before treating capture as done. Stop any other background candidate process you started.
7. Call `files` (or any analysis tool; they auto-discover) against the output directory `-d` names.

**Eager vs. HIP-graph attribution.** Check `graphs` before reading per-op
timing as ground truth: a small number of large graph-launch entries covering
decode means decode runs under a captured HIP graph, and per-op eager numbers
elsewhere in the trace do not attribute to it: use replay timing and
inter-replay scheduling gaps instead. One entry per op instead of graph-launch
entries means decode is eager; `idle_gaps` and `cpu_overhead` are the right
tools for launch-bound vs. kernel-bound there. Do not sum per-op eager timings
and call the result graph-era throughput.

